<section id="yaml-syntax" class="section">
  <div class="container-tool max-w-3xl">
    <div class="text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
      <h2 class="h-section mt-3">YAML Syntax Guide: A Complete Reference</h2>
      <p class="lead mt-4">Learn YAML syntax from the ground up — scalars, mappings, sequences, anchors, aliases, and best practices for clean config files.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6 text-zinc-600 leading-relaxed">
      <p>YAML (YAML Ain't Markup Language) is the most popular configuration language in the DevOps world. From Kubernetes manifests to CI/CD pipelines, understanding YAML syntax is essential for every modern developer.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">What Makes YAML Different?</h3>
      <p>Unlike JSON or XML, YAML uses <strong>indentation</strong> (spaces, never tabs) to represent structure. This makes it incredibly readable for humans but also means a single misplaced space can break your entire file. That's why a <a href="/#formatter">YAML formatter</a> is so valuable — it catches these issues instantly.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML Basic Building Blocks</h3>
      <p><strong>Scalars</strong> are the simplest YAML values: strings, numbers, booleans, and null.</p>
      <pre><code># Strings (quotes are optional unless containing special characters)
name: John Doe
description: "Value with: special chars"

# Numbers
age: 30
price: 19.99

# Booleans (YAML 1.2: only true/false)
enabled: true

# Null
middle_name: null</code></pre>

      <p><strong>Mappings</strong> map keys to values using colons.</p>
      <pre><code>person:
  name: Alice
  age: 28
  address:
    street: 123 Main St
    city: San Francisco</code></pre>

      <p><strong>Sequences</strong> are ordered lists, denoted by dashes.</p>
      <pre><code>fruits:
  - apple
  - banana
  - orange

# List of objects
users:
  - name: Alice
    role: admin
  - name: Bob
    role: developer</code></pre>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">Advanced YAML Features</h3>
      <p><strong>Anchors and Aliases:</strong> anchors (<code>&amp;</code>) mark a node for reuse, aliases (<code>*</code>) reference it — perfect for DRY configuration.</p>
      <pre><code>defaults: &amp;defaults
  timeout: 30
  retries: 3

service-a:
  &lt;&lt;: *defaults
  name: service-a</code></pre>

      <p><strong>Multi-Document Files:</strong> YAML files can contain multiple documents separated by <code>---</code>, common in Kubernetes manifests.</p>
      <pre><code>---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-service
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-deployment</code></pre>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">Common YAML Pitfalls</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Tabs vs Spaces:</strong> YAML <em>never</em> allows tabs for indentation. Use spaces only.</li>
        <li><strong>Inconsistent Indentation:</strong> Pick a consistent indent level (2 or 4 spaces) and stick with it.</li>
        <li><strong>Colon Spacing:</strong> Always add a space after the colon: <code>key: value</code> not <code>key:value</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Boolean Ambiguity:</strong> YAML 1.1 accepts <code>yes/no/on/off</code> as booleans. Always use <code>true/false</code> to avoid surprises.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple Documents:</strong> Remember that <code>---</code> starts a new document and <code>...</code> ends one.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML in Practice</h3>
      <p>YAML is the backbone of modern infrastructure configuration:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Kubernetes:</strong> Every resource (Deployment, Service, ConfigMap) is defined in YAML.</li>
        <li><strong>Docker Compose:</strong> <code>docker-compose.yml</code> defines multi-container applications.</li>
        <li><strong>Ansible:</strong> Playbooks and inventory files use YAML.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Actions:</strong> Workflow definitions in <code>.github/workflows/*.yml</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>OpenAPI/Swagger:</strong> API specifications in YAML format.</li>
        <li><strong>Helm Charts:</strong> Kubernetes package manager uses YAML templates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML vs JSON: When to Use Which</h3>
      <p>YAML is a superset of JSON — every valid JSON file is also valid YAML. <strong>Choose YAML</strong> for human-readable configuration files, comments, and anchors. <strong>Choose JSON</strong> for APIs, data exchange, and JavaScript applications. For a full comparison including TOML, see the <a href="#format-comparison" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline underline-offset-4">format comparison below</a>.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML 1.2 vs 1.1: Key Differences</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Booleans:</strong> YAML 1.1 accepts <code>yes/no/on/off/true/false</code>. YAML 1.2 only accepts <code>true/false</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Octal numbers:</strong> YAML 1.1 uses <code>0777</code> prefix; YAML 1.2 uses <code>0o777</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>String "NO"/"Yes":</strong> In YAML 1.1 these parse as booleans; in YAML 1.2 they stay strings.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When in doubt, quote ambiguous values: <code>answer: "yes"</code> ensures it's always a string.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML Best Practices</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Always use spaces for indentation</strong> — never tabs, and don't mix levels.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote strings that could be misinterpreted</strong> — like <code>"true"</code>, <code>"1.0"</code>, or version numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>Use comments liberally</strong> — YAML supports <code>#</code> comments.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep nesting shallow</strong> — deeply nested YAML is error-prone.</li>
        <li><strong>Use anchors for repeated blocks</strong> — <code>&amp;anchor</code> and <code>*alias</code> with <code>&lt;&lt;:</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate before committing</strong> — use a <a href="/#formatter">YAML validator</a> to catch syntax errors before pushing to production.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML Security Considerations</h3>
      <p>YAML deserialization can be a security risk if you parse untrusted input. Many libraries support arbitrary object construction through tags like <code>!!python/object</code>. To stay safe: never parse untrusted YAML with unsafe loaders — use <code>yaml.safe_load()</code> in Python, validate before processing, and be cautious with <code>!!</code> tags.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <p><strong>Is YAML case-sensitive?</strong> Yes. <code>Name: Alice</code> and <code>name: Alice</code> are different keys.</p>
      <p><strong>Can I use comments in YAML?</strong> Yes. Single-line comments start with <code>#</code>. Block comments are not supported.</p>
      <p><strong>What's the difference between | and &gt;?</strong> The pipe <code>|</code> preserves literal newlines; the greater-than <code>&gt;</code> folds newlines into spaces.</p>
      <p><strong>How do I represent a date in YAML?</strong> YAML recognizes ISO 8601 formats like <code>2024-01-15</code>. Quote them to keep them as strings.</p>
